              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Accounts.fs",
                "line": 33,
                "quote": "|> List.filter (fun (fine, covered) -> covered >= fine.Amount)"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The oldest-first allocation of payments to fines (`Accounts.allocation`) and the list of settled fines (`Accounts.settledCopies`) are in Tier 2, and Tier 3 calls them. But `Views.account` in Tier 3 also works out the fine's payment status (paid, unpaid or part-paid) with its own `covered >= fine.Amount` and `covered = 0m` tests. That repeats the 'fully paid' predicate that `settledCopies` already holds in Tier 2. The two agree, so this is a duplicate and not a contradiction.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The change adds no effects. `allocation` and `settledCopies` are pure functions in Tier 2. `ofPayment` is a pure projection that now takes the before and after libraries. The CLI only formats the Tier 3 view, and it calls no Transition function and no Persistence code.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 270,
                "quote": "if covered >= fine.Amount then \"paid\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Transition/Accounts.fs",
                "line": 33,
                "quote": "covered >= fine.Amount"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The fact 'a fine is fully paid' is written in two places, once in Tier 2 `settledCopies` and once in the Tier 3 status string. The copies agree today but could drift apart. The oldest-first allocation itself is defined once and used by `owed`, `account` and `pay`.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
