              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Host/Cli/CommandLine.fs",
                "line": 167,
                "quote": "if Accounts.isBlocked (MemberId m) library then"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The waiver model (`Waiver` in Tier 1), the `waive` guards and target selection (`Payments.waive`), and `Fines.payable` are all in Tier 2 or lower. Tier 3 still derives the fine status itself, with its own waived, paid and unpaid conditions on `covered`. That repeats rules held in Tier 2 (`settledCopies`, `waive`) without contradicting them. The CLI's borrow-blocking decision still sits in presentation code. It now asks Tier 2 `isBlocked` and branches on the answer, which fixes the earlier local 5.00 calculation.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Host/Cli/CommandLine.fs",
                "line": 167,
                "quote": "if Accounts.isBlocked (MemberId m) library then\n    Error(BorrowingBlocked(MemberId m)) |> render showBorrow"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The Tier 4 presentation module `Lending.Host.Cli` now calls `Lending.Transition.Accounts.isBlocked` directly and makes the borrow-blocking decision itself. It also loads the library through `ports.Store.Load()` instead of going through a use case. That is a new call from presentation into Tier 2 (the ARC-BND-02 pattern, and the rubric's 0 level). The Tier 2/3 code itself adds no new effects, and persistence of the waiver goes through the existing JSON codec, which is Tier 4 persistence. The CLI call is a new violation even though it replaced an older inline calculation.",
